<http://data.silknow.org/object/1785bf88-0a49-3eae-b79a-5de0fb1fdb72>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This sumptuous evening dress has huge puffed sleeves which were highly fashionable during the mid 1890s. One of the advantages of these large sleeves was to make the waist look small by comparison. The shoulder width was counterbalanced by the size of the skirt, which widened at the hem - an effect that was achieved by gores, shaped panels, box pleats in the back of the skirt and stiff interlining. \n\nThe skirt and bodice is embroidered in beads with exquisite butterfly and ribbon motifs. Butterflies were popular in 1894. The Queen in April illustrated an example by the couturier Felix.\n\nThe label 'Stern Bros., West 23rd , New York' is stitched to the waistband. Stern Bros., one of the largest New York department stores of the time, imported models of Parisian fashions for copying."@en , "Dress consisting of a bodice and skirt of embroidered silk velvet, possibly made in Paris, retailed by Stern Brothers, New York, 1894"@en , "Dress consisting of a bodice and skirt of blue silk velvet. It is trimmed with glass beads and sequin embroidery and bead-embroidered net with a design of butterflies. It is lined with black silk."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/e3ceae8b-8247-3fe7-9f5a-f55ddbf0ceb7> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/d1975e5c-f8f7-3a06-813b-8a90c733c275> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/de40441a-2c13-3558-95b8-fd9fa1041f2d> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/5e2b8734-c142-34b5-95b7-a0be5e6ada68> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/e48eef57-4e1d-38c9-9087-40095a797a4a> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This sumptuous evening dress has huge puffed sleeves which were highly fashionable during the mid 1890s. One of the advantages of these large sleeves was to make the waist look small by comparison. The shoulder width was counterbalanced by the size of the skirt, which widened at the hem - an effect that was achieved by gores, shaped panels, box pleats in the back of the skirt and stiff interlining. \n\nThe skirt and bodice is embroidered in beads with exquisite butterfly and ribbon motifs. Butterflies were popular in 1894. The Queen in April illustrated an example by the couturier Felix.\n\nThe label 'Stern Bros., West 23rd , New York' is stitched to the waistband. Stern Bros., one of the largest New York department stores of the time, imported models of Parisian fashions for copying."@en , "Dress consisting of a bodice and skirt of blue silk velvet. It is trimmed with glass beads and sequin embroidery and bead-embroidered net with a design of butterflies. It is lined with black silk."@en , "Dress consisting of a bodice and skirt of embroidered silk velvet, possibly made in Paris, retailed by Stern Brothers, New York, 1894"@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "T.272&A-1972" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O13858> .

